---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import BackLink from "../../../components/BackLink.astro";

const publishedDate = "2026-08-04";
const title = "Private AI for engineering and architecture firms — Zaviren";
const description =
	"Technical reports rewritten from scratch, tenders against the clock, and client NDAs that forbid moving the documentation. How AI running inside the practice helps.";

const faq = [
	{
		q: "Can it run calculations or produce drawings?",
		a: "No. It does not replace your calculation software, CAD or BIM. It is a documentation tool: it finds, compares and drafts from projects, reports and standards you have already written.",
	},
	{
		q: "What about the NDAs we sign with clients?",
		a: "That is the main reason this sector asks. Many contracts forbid passing project documentation to third parties without authorisation — and uploading it to a cloud AI is, literally, disclosure to a third party. With the server in the practice there is no disclosure to justify.",
	},
	{
		q: "Does it read drawings and technical PDFs?",
		a: "It reads text: PDFs, Word documents and Excel sheets — reports, tender documents, annexes and site records. It does not interpret the geometry of a drawing; that is what your technical tools are for.",
	},
	{
		q: "Is it useful for preparing tender submissions?",
		a: "It is one of the clearest returns: it retrieves the equivalent sections from your own previous submissions and prepares a draft the team reviews, instead of starting from a blank page each time.",
	},
	{
		q: "Do we have to reorganise the project archive first?",
		a: "You don't need to tidy it up: it indexes the folder structure exactly as it is — the mess is precisely the problem it solves. What does get reviewed at the start is where that archive lives and how it is set up, because that determines how it connects.",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	lang="en"
	title={title}
	description={description}
	altHref="/blog/ia-privada-ingenieria-arquitectura"
>
	<article>
		<section class="hero">
			<span class="eyebrow">Blog · Industry</span>
			<h1>Private AI for <span class="gradient">engineering and architecture</span></h1>
			<p class="subtitle">
				Twenty years of projects, reports and tender documents on the
				practice's server. Every new bid closely resembles an old one — but
				finding it costs more than rewriting it.
			</p>
			<div class="post-meta">
				<span class="badge">4 Aug 2026</span>
				<span class="badge">6 min read</span>
			</div>
			<div class="cta-row">
				<a class="cta" href="/en/contact">Tell us about your case</a>
				<a class="cta cta-secondary" href="/en/guide">Download the free guide</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="callout">
				<span class="eyebrow">In one sentence</span>
				<p>
					A technical consultancy's real asset is its project history, and it is
					almost never searchable. Private AI indexes reports, tender documents,
					annexes and site records, and answers citing the specific project and
					section. It runs on a server in the practice, so documentation covered
					by an NDA or by the client's intellectual property is never disclosed
					to an outside provider.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">01 · Day to day</span>
				<h2>The <span class="gradient">real pains</span></h2>
				<p>The ones that show up exactly when there's a deadline.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="pain-grid">
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">📐</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Rewriting what is already written</h3>
						<p>The report for a similar project exists, but finding it in the archive costs more than drafting the section again.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">⏱️</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Tenders against the clock</h3>
						<p>Hundreds of pages of requirements and a week to respond. Pinning down which ones apply burns half a day of a senior engineer.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">🧠</span>
					<div>
						<h3>The technical criteria are unwritten</h3>
						<p>How the practice resolves a recurring detail is known by three people, and they always have to be interrupted.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="pain-icon">🤐</span>
					<div>
						<h3>NDAs that block the obvious tool</h3>
						<p>The team would use cloud AI, but client contracts forbid passing project documentation to third parties.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">02 · The fix</span>
				<h2>What AI does <span class="gradient">about those four</span></h2>
			</div>
			<div class="cap-grid">
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">01</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Reuses your own history</h3>
						<p>"Building services reports for industrial buildings using this solution" retrieves the sections of earlier projects that cover exactly that, with the document cited.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">02</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Turns a tender document into answers</h3>
						<p>Ask about eligibility requirements, deadlines or scoring criteria and get the answer with the paragraph quoted.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">03</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Makes the practice's criteria searchable</h3>
						<p>Construction details, internal notes and documented decisions stop living only in the seniors' heads.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<span class="cap-num">04</span>
					<div>
						<h3>Fits the NDAs you already signed</h3>
						<p>With nothing leaving the practice, there is no third-party disclosure to authorise and no confidentiality clause at risk.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="split">
				<div class="copy">
					<span class="eyebrow">03 · Why private</span>
					<h2>The project <span class="gradient">isn't yours to disclose</span></h2>
					<p>
						In engineering and architecture the problem isn't only data
						protection: it's contractual. Much of a project's documentation is
						covered by confidentiality agreements and by the client's
						intellectual property. Uploading it to a cloud tool is disclosure to
						a third party, enterprise plan or not.
					</p>
					<p>
						There, <a href="/en/private-ai">private AI</a> isn't a preference —
						it's the only thing that fits: the model runs on a server in the
						practice, nothing reaches the internet, and you can answer in
						writing when a client asks where their documentation ends up.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="illustration">
					<img src="/images/chat-with-ai.svg" alt="Asking questions in plain language over the project archive" width="400" height="262" />
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">04 · Limits</span>
				<h2>What it does <span class="gradient">not</span> do</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					It does not calculate, model or draw: it replaces neither calculation
					software nor CAD or BIM, and it does not interpret the geometry of a
					drawing. It signs nothing and takes on no technical responsibility —
					review by a qualified engineer or architect remains mandatory. And it
					knows no standard you never indexed: it works over your own archive,
					not over an external body of regulations. And it produces no exhaustive
					listings ("give me every project using this solution"): it answers over
					the documents it retrieves for each question, not by sweeping the whole
					collection.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="block-head">
				<span class="eyebrow">05 · FAQ</span>
				<h2>Before you <span class="gradient">ask</span></h2>
			</div>
			<div class="faq-list">
				{faq.map((item) => (
					<details class="faq-item">
						<summary>{item.q}</summary>
						<p>{item.a}</p>
					</details>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="final-cta">
				<div>
					<h2>Let's start with the next tender</h2>
					<p>A 30-minute call to see which part of the project archive is worth indexing first.</p>
				</div>
				<a class="cta" href="/en/contact">Book a call</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<div class="post-footer-links">
			<BackLink lang="en" />
			<a class="back-link" href="/en/blog/private-ai-vs-chatgpt">Read more: Private AI vs ChatGPT →</a>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>

<style>
	.post-meta {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin-top: 1.2rem;
	}
	.post-meta .badge {
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.callout {
		max-width: 720px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
		background: var(--card);
		border-radius: 16px;
		padding: 1.6rem 1.8rem;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.6rem;
	}
	.callout p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
	}
	.prose {
		max-width: 720px;
		margin: 0 auto;
	}
	.prose p {
		margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
	}
	.prose p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}
	.prose a,
	.copy a {
		color: inherit;
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-color: var(--border);
		text-underline-offset: 3px;
	}
	.prose a:hover,
	.copy a:hover {
		text-decoration-color: currentColor;
	}
	.post-footer-links {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		align-items: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}
</style>

<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "BlogPosting",
	headline: "Private AI for engineering and architecture firms",
	description,
	datePublished: publishedDate,
	dateModified: publishedDate,
	author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Zaviren" },
	publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Zaviren" },
	mainEntityOfPage: "https://zaviren.com/en/blog/private-ai-engineering-architecture-firms",
})} />

<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "FAQPage",
	mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({
		"@type": "Question",
		name: item.q,
		acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
	})),
})} />
